---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const stages = [
  {
    title: 'Fetch',
    body: 'The nightly GitHub Action finds every public repo where your account made commits in the last 30 days, exports each commit with its full diff, scans the export for secrets, and uploads it as an artifact that expires after 10 days.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Select',
    body: 'Commits already used are skipped, as are commits from denylisted repos and trivial ones such as lockfile-only or formatting-only changes. The rest are ranked by message quality, diff size, new files, and whether behavior changes.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Decide the mode',
    body: 'If at least one story clears the score threshold, shipnote writes a story post about it. Otherwise it writes one theme post and no tweets.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Draft',
    body: 'The story or theme is sent to the agent you configure, along with voice samples and recent posts from agent-brain. The agent returns structured JSON, so every draft can be checked by code.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Validate',
    body: 'Every cited commit SHA must exist in the dataset, and every named file, function, or number must appear in that commit. Tweets must fit 280 characters including links. Claims that fail are dropped and logged.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Review and approve',
    body: 'You see each draft beside its evidence and approve, edit, or reject it. Nothing is marked as used until you run the command that does so, and nothing posts automatically.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="How it works: shipnote"
  description="How shipnote turns your public commits into tweets and blog drafts, with every claim checked against the commit it came from."
>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-20 pb-24">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How it works</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-zinc-400">
      shipnote runs in two parts: a nightly export that runs on GitHub, and a drafting step you run yourself. Each stage below either succeeds with evidence or stops and tells you why.
    </p>

    <ol class="mt-14 space-y-6">
      {stages.map((stage, i) => (
        <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/[0.08] bg-zinc-950 p-6">
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-sky-400">0{i + 1}</span>
            <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">{stage.title}</h2>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">{stage.body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <div class="mt-14 rounded-xl border border-white/[0.08] p-6">
      <h2 class="font-semibold">When something fails</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
        If the model is down, the dataset is partial, or every draft fails validation, shipnote writes a review file that explains what happened and produces no drafts. It does not retry forever or post a weaker substitute.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
